docs/plans/lobu-apply-drift-gate.md:55:- **`owned`** — the set of **kind-qualified definition incarnation identities** `{ kind, id }` (kind ∈ entity-type | relationship-type | automation | connector-definition; ids from independent domains: `entity_types.id`, `entity_relationship_types.id`, `automation_id`, `connector_definitions.id`) **this config actually applied** (delete-eligible). Qualifying by kind is load-bearing: the ID domains are independent, so an unqualified set can collide across kinds (e.g. `entity_types.id = 5` and `automation_id = 5`) and misclassify a UI-created definition as owned. Storing `{ kind, id }`, never slugs or connector key+version, is what makes delete eligibility sound: a config-expressed delete that succeeds and then has its summary POST fail leaves the identity in `owned`; if the user re-creates the same slug (or re-installs the same connector key/version — uninstall archives the row, so a reinstall is a **new `connector_definitions.id`**) in the UI, the new row has a **new id** and is therefore **not owned** — it blocks as drift instead of being auto-deleted under `--yes`.
